    <div class="report-prose paper-abstract">
      <h2>Abstract</h2>
      <p>
        Frontier multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can control robots without robot-specific
        training, but how their capabilities compare and interact with post-trained
        vision-language-action (VLA) and world action model (WAM) policies across manipulation
        demands remains to be thoroughly evaluated. In this report, we evaluate GPT-6-Astra on
        EBench as the core of an agent that controls a robot through an agent harness under the same
        visual and proprioceptive observation protocol as post-trained policies. We study two agent
        configurations: direct control, in which the agent generates waypoint commands directly, and
        harness with policy, in which it can also invoke a post-trained VLA or WAM policy for action
        execution. Under direct control, the agent achieves competitive overall performance relative
        to post-trained policies. It leads on short-horizon tasks and exhibits the least performance
        variation across generalization dimensions, whereas it underperforms post-trained policies
        on long-horizon tasks, tabletop manipulation, and high-precision contact-rich tasks. In the
        harness with policy, invoking a post-trained VLA or WAM combines the complementary strengths
        of both components, raising overall task success to the level of the strongest compared
        post-trained policies while substantially reducing token cost. However, performance remains
        below the per-task upper bound of the standalone components due to suboptimal policy
        invocation, fixed-instruction execution without subgoals, and grasp disruption when the
        agent resumes control. Overall, our study characterizes the capability boundary of a
        frontier MLLM agent and suggests directions for improving future agent harness design.
      </p>
